🧠 Introduction

The Java Collections Framework (JCF) provides a set of interfaces and classes for storing and manipulating data efficiently.

👉 Understanding collection interfaces and their implementations is essential for:

✔ DSA preparation
✔ Java interviews
✔ Real-world application development

---

🏗️ Collection Framework Hierarchy

```text id="c1"
Iterable
   │
Collection
   │
├── List
├── Set
└── Queue

Map (Separate Interface)
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 1. List Interface

A List stores elements in an ordered sequence.

---

📌 Features

✔ Maintains insertion order

✔ Allows duplicate elements

✔ Supports index-based access

---

📌 Common Implementations

* ArrayList
* LinkedList
* Vector
* Stack

---

💻 Example

```java id="l1"
import java.util.*;

List<String> list =
        new ArrayList<>();

list.add("Java");
list.add("Python");
list.add("Java");

System.out.println(list);
```

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 2. Set Interface

A Set stores unique elements.

---

📌 Features

✔ No duplicate values

✔ Faster searching
✔ Useful for uniqueness checks

---

📌 Common Implementations

* HashSet
* LinkedHashSet
* TreeSet

---

💻 Example

```java id="s1"
Set<Integer> set =
        new HashSet<>();

set.add(10);
set.add(20);
set.add(10);

System.out.println(set);
```

Output:

```text id="s2"
[10, 20]
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 3. Queue Interface

Queue follows FIFO (First In First Out).

---

📌 Features

✔ Sequential processing

✔ Efficient insertion/removal

---

📌 Common Implementations

* LinkedList
* PriorityQueue
* ArrayDeque

---

💻 Example

```java id="q1"
Queue<Integer> queue =
        new LinkedList<>();

queue.add(10);
queue.add(20);

System.out.println(queue.poll());
```

Output:

```text id="q2"
10
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 4. Deque Interface

Deque stands for Double-Ended Queue.

---

📌 Features

✔ Insert/remove from both ends

✔ Supports stack and queue operations

---

📌 Implementations

* ArrayDeque
* LinkedList

---

💻 Example

```java id="d1"
Deque<Integer> deque =
        new ArrayDeque<>();

deque.addFirst(10);
deque.addLast(20);

System.out.println(deque);
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 5. Map Interface

Stores data as key-value pairs.

👉 Map is not part of Collection interface.

---

📌 Features

✔ Unique keys
✔ Fast retrieval
✔ Key-value mapping

---

📌 Common Implementations

* HashMap
* LinkedHashMap
* TreeMap
* Hashtable

---

💻 Example

```java id="m1"
Map<Integer, String> map =
        new HashMap<>();

map.put(1, "Java");
map.put(2, "Python");

System.out.println(map);
```

---

📊 Core Implementations Comparison

| Interface | Implementation | Ordered        | Duplicates  |
| --------- | -------------- | -------------- | ----------- |
| List      | ArrayList      | ✔ Yes          | ✔ Yes       |
| List      | LinkedList     | ✔ Yes          | ✔ Yes       |
| Set       | HashSet        | ❌ No          | ❌ No       |
| Set       | LinkedHashSet  | ✔ Yes          | ❌ No       |
| Set       | TreeSet        | Sorted         | ❌ No       |
| Queue     | PriorityQueue  | Priority Based | ✔ Yes       |
| Map       | HashMap        | ❌ No          | Keys Unique|
| Map       | TreeMap        | Sorted Keys    | Keys Unique |

